      "document_name": "os_interrupt_locks.md",
      "page": 1,
      "text": "The interaction of spinlocks and interrupts raises a potential danger. Suppose sys_pause holds tickslock, and its CPU is interrupted by a timer interrupt. clockintr would try to acquire tickslock, see it was held, and wait for it to be released. In this situation, tickslock will never be released: only sys_pause can release it, but sys_pause will not continue running until clockintr returns. So the CPU will deadlock, and any code that needs either lock will also freeze.\n\nTo avoid this situation, if a spinlock is used by an interrupt handler, a CPU must never hold that lock with interrupts enabled. Xv6 is more conservative: when a CPU acquires any lock, xv6 always disables interrupts on that CPU. Interrupts may still occur on other CPUs, so an interrupt’s acquire can wait for a thread to release a spinlock; just not on the same CPU.",
